.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
\fBModel\fP topology associates elements with regions and boundaries, recording their names, element types, and their dimensionality\&. This is important in model construction process\&. The \fBModelTopology\fP thus stores this "topologic" information, which cannot be stored in the \fBVSet\fP\&.

.PP
In CSMP regions are model subdomains, just as boundaries and split boundaries\&. All of these can be stored in the \fBModelTopology\fP\&. They are distinguished only by their names\&. Thus, a \fBBoundary\fP either contains the string BOUNDARY in its name or has one of the standard names for boundaries and or edges of box-shaped models (BOTTOM, TOP, LEFT, RIGHT, BACK, FRONT, IRREGULAR etc\&., EDGE1\&.\&.EDGE12\&. Likewise, \fBSplitBoundary\fP objects contain the string SPLITBOUNDARY in their name, typically separated by underscores from other more specific parts of the name\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
It follows that the user has to assign appropriate names to the geometrical entities in their model that they want to become boundaries\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBModel\fP topology can also be used to perform a consistency check on the cells stored in the \fBVSet\fP\&. Thus, it can check the cell numbering and the neighbor connectivity\&. To some degree it can also fix such potential problems with the \fBVSet\fP\&.

.PP
When the model is supposed box-shaped, this is tested and potentially missing information is restored in collaboration with the \fBVSet\fP / \fBVData\fP and \fBBox\fP\&. In this case it can deduce the correct \fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP flags and assign them to the nodes\&.

.PP
When a model is created by ANSYS and output using the CSMP interface of ANSYS the information needed to initialise the ModelToplogy class is contained in the '\&.asc' file\&.
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
\fBModelTopology\fP was created to convey topological information from an ANSYS model to CSMP so that corresponding named groups of elements can be created and assigned specific material properties\&. 
.br

.PP
For this purpose, \fBModelTopology\fP stores named maps of the element IDs and types that make up specific model regions\&. By default, the element names are the ANSYS element names as captured inside the class \fBANSYS_ElementSpecifications\fP\&. Functionality is there in the public interface to convert these into CSMP names\&.

.PP
Since one does not always want to use all topological information and or finite elements in a simulation, \fBModelTopology\fP allows the user to reduce the initial topology as, for instance, obtained from ANSYS's meshing tools, to a few target regions - or element types, such as only the volume elements\&. Several interfaces are provided for this purpose\&. 
.br
.SH "Structure"
.PP
\fBModelTopology\fP assumes a complementary role to the \fBVSet\fP which stores the connectivity between elements and their nodes\&. Thus, there must always be a supporting \fBVSet\fP in order to build a \fBModel\fP\&. When the \fBModelTopology\fP is reduced to a subset of the original model, corresponding operations must be performed on the \fBVSet\fP\&.

.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthaei 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
2001 
.RE
.PP

