.SS "void csmp::ModelTopology::EliminateCellType (const char * etype)"
Gets rid of all the model regions which only contain the target element type\&. For poly-element-type model regions the method cannot determine which of the elements are of the target type\&. Thus, they are left untouched\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIetype\fP A character string that matches the element type that is to be removed\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method reports back which regions are being eliminated\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ModelTopology::EliminateCellTypes (const std::list< std::string > & etypes)"
Removes the enlisted element types from the current model topology, irrespective whether this breaks the model up into spatially non- contiguous domains\&.

.PP
\fBEliminateCellTypes()\fP will leave regions which consist of a range of elements untouched, since it cannot identify which element IDs correspond to the enlisted types\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIetypes\fP The method takes a list of strings as argument which define the ANSYS element types\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use with utmost care !

.PP
RenumberCellsConsecutively() should be called after the element elimination to re-establish contiguous element numbering in the model\&. Corresponding operations must also be carried out on the \fBVSet\fP that stores the element connectivity which has to be updated when elements were removed\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ModelTopology::EliminateLineCells ()"

.PP
remove all curves Removes all line elements from the current model\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use carefully ! - while the nodes of line elements are typically shared with surface or volume elements, if single line wells are present in a model, the geometry is disrupted\&.

.PP
RenumberCellsConsecutively() should be called after the element elimination to re-establish contiguous element numbering in the model\&. Corresponding operations must also be carried out on the \fBVSet\fP that stores the element connectivity which has to be updated when elements were removed\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ModelTopology::EliminateSurfaceCells ()"

.PP
remove all surfaces Removes all surface elements from the current topology\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
It may be desirable to get rid of internal or external surfaces in a model since their accumulation will add extra terms to the solution of PDE integrals\&. If the surface element nodes are shared with volume elements, the model topology will stay intact\&. Else the model will be broken\&.

.PP
RenumberCellsConsecutively() should be called after the element elimination to re-establish contiguous element numbering in the model\&. Corresponding operations must also be carried out on the \fBVSet\fP that stores the element connectivity which has to be updated when elements were removed\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ModelTopology::EliminateVolumeCells ()"

.PP
remove all volumetric elements from a model Method eliminates all volume elements from the current model topology\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
This operation typically greatly upsets a current model, but it gives the option to do, for instance, a fracture only model of a volumetric mesh of a fractured rock\&. It is recommended, however, to control this inside the meshing tool rather than by post-processing, because a better and smaller mesh can typically be created if it is known that only surfaces are required\&.

.PP
RenumberCellsConsecutively() should be called after the element elimination to re-establish contiguous element numbering in the model\&. Corresponding operations must also be carried out on the \fBVSet\fP that stores the element connectivity which has to be updated when elements were removed\&. 
.SS "void csmp::ModelTopology::Erase ()"

.PP
remove certain regions all regions

.PP
Returns all the dynamically allocated memory to the operating system\&. Consequently all the information about the current model is lost\&.

.PP
The name of the model topology is set to 'erased' to signify that all data are gone\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
